  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "US bans use of China’s DeepSeek AI tools by government agencies",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/20/us-bans-china-deepseek-ai-tools-government-agencies",
      "description": "<p>Use of tools ‘may result in the transfer of sensitive data’ to China, defense department says</p><p>The <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news\">US</a> government on Monday ordered its agencies to stop using artificial intelligence (AI) tools from the Chinese startup <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/deepseek\">DeepSeek</a>, as the countries race for dominance in the fast-moving sector.</p><p>A notice from the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) announced the ban on products developed by DeepSeek – and tools created using DeepSeek by third parties – saying they must be removed from IT devices.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/20/us-bans-china-deepseek-ai-tools-government-agencies\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-20T12:33:27"
    },
    {
      "title": "China defends DeepSeek after US ban of its AI from government systems",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/china-defends-deepseek-after-us-ban-of-its-ai-from-government-systems-126102000438_1.html",
      "description": "China hit back Tuesday at a US ban on Chinese artificial intelligence startup DeepSeek's products for federal civilian agencies, highlighting friction in the two AI powers' heated technology race.\nThe agencies should remove DeepSeek and its affiliated products, as well as third party products and services developed using DeepSeek, from their IT systems, the Department of Homeland Security said in a notice made public on Monday.\nThe department's order, dating back to late July, also blocks civilian agencies from procuring DeepSeek products and services. DeepSeek is one of China's leading and best-known AI companies and its open-source models are widely available in countries including the United States. They are mostly free to use.\nThe notice in the Federal Register alleges that DeepSeek's models \"present multiple threat vectors.\" It said concerns include that its servers are located in China and that Chinese authorities' could potentially access private company infrastructure and ..",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-20T10:18:41"
    },
    {
      "title": "US bans Chinese AI firm DeepSeek from government agency IT systems",
